<script>
	import Callout from '$lib/components/Callout.svelte';
</script>

Parameterized tests are where file-based testing pulls ahead of click-ops. Point one
request at a dataset and ApiTool runs it once per row, turning every column into a
variable. Here we provision a batch of users — seeding any missing fields with
**faker**, **filtering** the dataset, **rate-limiting** the run, and running
iterations **in parallel**.

## The dataset

```csv
email,role,department,active
alice@example.com,admin,platform,true
bob@example.com,user,sales,true
carol@example.com,viewer,sales,false
dave@example.com,user,platform,true
```

## The collection

The `data_driven` block controls the whole run. Columns become `{{variables}}`;
anything not in the dataset can be generated. Iteration metadata is exposed as
`{{_index}}`, `{{_count}}`, and `{{_total}}`.

```yaml
# collections/provision.yaml
name: Provision users
requests:
  - name: Create user
    data_driven:
      source: "./data/users.csv"
      filter: "{{active}} == 'true'"   # only provision active rows
      parallel: true                    # run iterations concurrently (up to 20 workers)
      rate_limit_rps: 5                 # ...but never exceed 5 req/s, enforced across workers
      fail_fast: false                  # report every failure, don't stop at the first
      store_results: all                # keep per-iteration detail in the report
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{base_url}}/admin/users"
      body:
        email: "{{email}}"
        role: "{{role}}"
        department: "{{department}}"
        # Fields absent from the CSV — generated fresh per row:
        display_name: "{{$faker.fullName}}"
        employee_id: "{{$uuid}}"
        phone: "{{$faker.phone}}"
    assertions:
      status: 201
      body:
        "$.email":
          equals: "{{email}}"
        "$.id":
          type: "string"
```

## Run it

```bash
apitest run collections/provision.yaml
```

## What you get

The `viewer` row is filtered out (`active == false`); the remaining three run
concurrently, paced to 5 req/s, and roll up into a single summary.

```
Collection: Provision users

Data-Driven: Create user (3 iterations)
  ✓ Iteration 1: Create user [1/3] (112ms)
  ✓ Iteration 2: Create user [2/3] (118ms)
  ✓ Iteration 3: Create user [3/3] (121ms)

  Summary: 3 passed, 0 failed (3 total)
  Average duration: 117ms

────────────────────────────────
  1 request(s): 3 passed, 0 failed (351ms)
```

<Callout type="output">
	Representative output — this collection targets a private admin API. The
	<code>[i/N]</code> naming, the filtered count, and the aggregated summary match
	ApiTool's documented data-driven format.
</Callout>

## Scaling up safely

JSON and YAML datasets work identically — just point `source:` at a `.json` array or
`.yaml` sequence. Need to slice a huge file? Use `start_row` / `end_row` / `limit`.

<Callout type="warn">
	Datasets over <strong>10,000 rows</strong> require an explicit
	<code>--confirm-large-dataset</code> flag, so you never accidentally fire 50k
	requests at a real API. Without it, the run exits with code 2.
</Callout>
